\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Proposition}
\title{Checkerboard elimination creates two new couplings}
\date{2026-11-06}

\p{Let #{L\ge 6} be even, and let the vertices be the torus
#{\mathbb Z/L\mathbb Z\times\mathbb Z/L\mathbb Z}. Equip the real
functions on these vertices with the counting inner product
#{h}.
Let #{a\neq 0} and #{b\neq 0}. Let #{T_1} and #{T_2} be the
unit shifts #{(T_1f)(x,y)=f(x-1,y)} and
#{(T_2f)(x,y)=f(x,y-1)}, and define
##{Q=aI-b(T_1+T_1^{-1}+T_2+T_2^{-1}).}
Let #{X} and #{Y} be the canonical subspaces of functions
supported on the even-parity sites and the odd-parity sites.
Let #{\iota_X}, #{\iota_Y}, #{\pi_X} and #{\pi_Y} be the
corresponding inclusions and projections, and let
#{M=\pi_XQ\iota_Y} be the off-diagonal map from #{Y} to #{X}.
Write #{M^{\dagger}} for its counting adjoint. The
\vocabk{Schur complement}{fdip-000D} that eliminates the odd
class is the operator
##{S=aI-a^{-1}MM^{\dagger}}
on #{X}. For the canonical indicator #{e_x} of an even site,
the kernel evaluations are #{h(e_x,Se_x)=a-4b^2/a},
#{h(e_x,Se_{x+v})=-b^2/a} at each axial offset
#{v=\pm(2,0)} or #{v=\pm(0,2)}, and
#{h(e_x,Se_{x+v})=-2b^2/a} at each diagonal offset
#{v=(\pm 1,\pm 1)}. For #{L\ge 6} these offsets are distinct
on the torus. When #{Q} is a
\vocabk{positive precision parameter}{fdip-0004}, this
complement is the precision of the retained Gaussian marginal.}

\p{Remark. In the counting basis of site indicators, #{Q} has
diagonal #{a} and nearest-neighbor entries #{-b}.}

\proof{
\p{The shifts #{T_1} and #{T_2} exchange the two parity classes,
so each class induces an empty graph. Declare the counting
basis of site indicators, with the even class written first.
In that basis both diagonal blocks of #{Q} equal #{a} times
the identity, and the off-diagonal block is the matrix of
#{M}. The counting adjoint #{M^{\dagger}} has that block's transpose
as its matrix. The Schur complement on the even class is
therefore #{aI-a^{-1}MM^{\dagger}}. The product of the off-diagonal
block with its transpose, at a pair of even sites, equals
#{b^2} times the number of common odd neighbors, and #{S}
subtracts #{a^{-1}} times that product. An even
vertex has #{4} neighbors, so the kernel value on one
canonical indicator against itself is #{a-4b^2/a}. The
vertices separated by #{(2,0)} share exactly one odd neighbor,
and likewise those separated by #{(0,2)}, so each axial
coefficient is #{-b^2/a}. The vertices separated by #{(1,1)}
share exactly two, and likewise those separated by #{(1,-1)},
so each diagonal-offset coefficient is #{-2b^2/a}. Since
#{M^{\dagger}} is the counting adjoint, #{S} is self-adjoint, and the
kernel takes the same coefficients at the opposite signs. For #{L\ge 6} the offsets
#{\pm(2,0)}, #{\pm(0,2)} and #{(\pm 1,\pm 1)} are distinct on
the torus. If #{Q} is a positive precision parameter,
\ref{fdip-000D} identifies the complement with the precision
of the marginal.}
}
